  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 222 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nAn overall drying and warming trend will return for the weekend into \nMonday. There will still be some scattered thunderstorms across the \nsoutheast mountains with isolated storms for the adjacent lower \nelevations and I-25 corridor region.  But with surface dew points \nand precipitable waters continuing to decrease, overall coverage \nand storm strengths should decrease.  \n\nSunday and Monday dry out and heat up.  Temperatures will top out \nover 100 across the southeast plains, and portions of the area may \nneed heat advisories one or both days.  We could see some spotty \nelevated fire weather conditions both days as humidities drop \nbelow 15 percent. But wind gusts appear to stay under critical \nthresholds for Red Flag criteria, so just hot and dry will be\nthe main story.\n\nA passing trough along the northern CONUS tier will send a cold \nfront into the southeast plains on Tuesday and this will bring \nanother upswing in thunderstorm chances to the southeast mountains \nand plains in post frontal upslope flow. Once again, strong \nstorms will be possible, bringing the risk of flash flooding for\nthe Aspen Acres burn scar. \n\nThe atmosphere gradually moistens through the end of the week as \nmodest monsoon moisture recirculates through the upper high \nacross the southwest U.S. Thunderstorm chances gradually \nincrease again over the mountains though best precipitation \nchances stay across the eastern half of the area.  Dew points \ntake longer to rebound across the western mountains as flow \naloft stays northwesterly. Overall will maintain thunderstorm \nchances every day, though suspect these will be less widespread \nand less efficient rain makers across the central mountains \nuntil we can get a more southerly monsoon tap back over the \nregion. -KT",
